#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124433 :question/id #uuid "c1120305-c8cb-5f57-97b1-9b31dc059b4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9961" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\det(A) = 0$\n\nII. $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n$\n\nIII. $A$ has $n$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible ![](images/dc/dc72aea1969143a926fd637d3360bb42116b27545a748923069f4ae732bd700f.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\det(A) = 0.$* $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nSince $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ must be invertible, which means $\\det(A) \\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n.$* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $A$ has $n$ pivot columns.* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=n,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $n$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124435 :question/id #uuid "2086adfe-9a09-5b60-8f5e-9edb0c700b98" :question/math-academy-id "e-9985" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3\\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in  \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\mathbb{R}^3$\n\nII. $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})\\ne\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{T}$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) ![](images/d5/d54f9467c22c22ade32bbb49f611c545140b9ce5d11610c04dd7f946d83ac789.svg) \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in  \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ we must have that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the vector $[1, 2, 1]^T$ does not lie in $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$, then $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3,$ the transformation $\\mathbf T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a2da09-ade9-5f02-bc30-6ec862b4c502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399728} {:answer/id #uuid "1f57ef16-daf6-55cf-b3cb-f0b639c205a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399729} {:answer/id #uuid "d2cdee49-7ce2-5cce-acee-cd378fd6b19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.5$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399730} {:answer/id #uuid "4c833b2d-9013-504c-b338-e5d0dc7d7a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399731} {:answer/id #uuid "11a1059c-7f59-5f04-b1af-4e76b82a2739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.5$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399728} :answer-field/id #uuid "6866c1e5-6616-5f78-9eb2-94b16182edd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399727}] :question/id #uuid "57c92fa7-df07-5594-a6b7-bd705bfe80a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-111172" :question/problem "The migration table below describes the movement of the population between cities and suburbs every year in a certain state. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Cities** | **Suburbs** |\n| To: | **Cities** | $0.8$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Suburbs** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |  |\n\nThat is, $20\\%$ of those living in cities move to the suburbs each year, and $70\\%$ of those living in suburbs move to the cities.\n\nAssuming that the population of the state is $9$ million and remains constant, how many people would eventually live in cities if the given probabilities remain constant over many years?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{7}{9}$ of the $9$ million population will live in cities. Therefore, the total number of people living in cities after many years will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot 9\\,\\text{million}=7\\,\\text{million}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "327c20aa-5b6d-5d5d-95d5-67fe1edd6dda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244765} {:answer/id #uuid "33ffc812-4891-586d-bc0d-281bd530059e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244781} {:answer/id #uuid "be00ba0f-33e3-51bb-b788-c863cec54aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246007} {:answer/id #uuid "beae8b6f-7eef-5f2b-b111-68f42f84b6fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246016} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a13eed-4a8b-5532-9d37-57dbd1d42511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246016} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb514e9d-3fa7-5bab-af7e-44674ca75bc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246913}] :question/id #uuid "cac3744a-0cdf-56d6-88ab-9bba2eefa254" :question/math-academy-id "q-111192" :question/problem "A particular town has a fleet of $720$ bicycles for rent. The table below gives the pattern of rental and return locations over a typical day. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Museum** | **Station** | **Gym** |\n| To: | **Museum** | $0.9$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| **Station** | $0$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |  |\n| **Gym** | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nFor example, $40\\%$ of the bicycles at the Station are moved to the Gym every day.\n\nHow many bicycles would eventually be located at the Station if the listed probabilities remained constant over many days?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.3 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.3 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & -0.7 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & -0.8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.3 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.7 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & -0.8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+3({x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 6{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many days, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the bicycles will be at the station. Therefore, the total number of bikes at the station after many days will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 720=120.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124788 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "848e6076-4e56-59d8-ab84-6808bf321d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "280000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121326} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcf50f0-b54f-5b81-ac83-38f4c029b097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "290000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121327} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1bfe76-520c-5c10-b350-34d63223b7e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121328} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3baefa-8431-5777-b25e-a8c744de4e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "350000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121329} {:answer/id #uuid "6e96dbb2-a51b-5a98-9932-cfb97c3b4f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "320000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121330}] :answer-field/id #uuid "876af953-f2d1-5ba4-815e-383a7226bac2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121325}] :question/id #uuid "c4d4936a-631d-5b30-8f00-e65967e70fc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-135462" :question/problem "A certain company produces three types of cars: Standard, Sports, and Deluxe. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of cars each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Standard** | **Sports** | **Deluxe** |\n| To: | **Standard** | $0.6$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n| **Sports** | $0.2$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |  |\n| **Deluxe** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |  |\n\nFor example, $20\\%$ of Standard customers switch to Sports each year, and $30\\%$ of Sports customers switch to Deluxe.\n\nCurrently, $500000$ customers use Standard, $400000$ customers use Sports and $100000$ customers use Deluxe. How many customers will use Sports in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dd8ae1f-e8a8-5241-9045-e9272d2fa0a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Trains** | **Cars** |\n| To: | **Trains** | $0.58$ | $0.33$ |\n| **Cars** | $0.42$ | $0.67$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122073} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f685fb-39a2-5e2f-bbdc-5f9b124ccc95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Trains** | **Cars** |\n| To: | **Trains** | $0.42$ | $0.67$ |\n| **Cars** | $0.58$ | $0.33$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122074} {:answer/id #uuid "f29284c7-a0a9-5e31-b16c-ad00e206433c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Trains** | **Cars** |\n| To: | **Trains** | $0.67$ | $0.58$ |\n| **Cars** | $0.33$ | $0.42$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122075} {:answer/id #uuid "50aede74-e258-5097-be49-da0bb8cf5bf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Trains** | **Cars** |\n| To: | **Trains** | $0.58$ | $0.67$ |\n| **Cars** | $0.42$ | $0.33$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122076} {:answer/id #uuid "575b68e8-51ea-5d28-8ebc-32fb86afe0a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Trains** | **Cars** |\n| To: | **Trains** | $0.42$ | $0.33$ |\n| **Cars** | $0.58$ | $0.67$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122077}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cceae898-e726-510e-b8d3-1a090c8bdbb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122072} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6178085f-74d2-5c03-ac64-810657822d5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Trains** | **Cars** |\n| To: | **Trains** | $0.58$ | $0.67$ |\n| **Cars** | $0.42$ | $0.33$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379774} {:answer/id #uuid "7cfe2466-8911-5393-acae-37ba283a1ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Trains** | **Cars** |\n| To: | **Trains** | $0.42$ | $0.33$ |\n| **Cars** | $0.58$ | $0.67$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379775} {:answer/id #uuid "b36522d6-f344-5f68-a8f9-f5b5f1b5fa91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Trains** | **Cars** |\n| To: | **Trains** | $0.58$ | $0.33$ |\n| **Cars** | $0.42$ | $0.67$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379776} {:answer/id #uuid "394c4d08-f75d-5273-a2ba-0855ad6dc82c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Trains** | **Cars** |\n| To: | **Trains** | $0.42$ | $0.67$ |\n| **Cars** | $0.58$ | $0.33$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379777} {:answer/id #uuid "80b9acca-9fa4-58b6-aa49-2d9e3782664e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Trains** | **Cars** |\n| To: | **Trains** | $0.67$ | $0.58$ |\n| **Cars** | $0.33$ | $0.42$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Trains** | **Cars** |\n| To: | **Trains** | $0.58$ | $0.33$ |\n| **Cars** | $0.42$ | $0.67$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379776} :answer-field/id #uuid "58a1eda3-e0ea-5cdd-878b-61ab3abea02a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379773}] :question/id #uuid "78ea34aa-4300-5834-9be1-24f93a7226aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-35787" :question/problem "In a particular country, there are two groups of people: those who use trains to travel and those who use cars. A study has shown that, each year \n\n- $42\\%$ of people who used trains swap to cars, and\n\n- $33\\%$ of people who used cars swap to trains.\n\nWhich of the following tables describes how these people change their preferences each year?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each year: \n\n- $42\\%$ of people who used trains swap to cars. This implies that $58\\%$ of them remain using trains.\n\n- $33\\%$ of people who used cars swap to trains. This implies that $67\\%$ of them remain using cars.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Trains** | **Cars** |\n| To: | **Trains** | $0.58$ | $0.33$ |\n| **Cars** | $0.42$ | $0.67$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.58 & 0.33 \\\\ 0.42 & 0.67\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9daa07dd-689b-5d68-a711-77459bc9f7e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122079} {:answer/id #uuid "c40ed3ab-fd7f-589e-9bca-e8611878bb22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.07" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122080} {:answer/id #uuid "22e0d828-15f0-56a1-944a-243f28fd0241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122081} {:answer/id #uuid "44d59735-fe69-523e-810e-154c352fc2f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.93" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122082} {:answer/id #uuid "45ca4e96-0cfd-54e5-a7fc-d0e7ec0255f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.93" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122082} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3b787ef-0c6d-5613-ae18-c431681bcaa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122078}] :question/id #uuid "f54c1f29-b43d-5f0e-a558-1fd0e7509f92" :question/math-academy-id "q-35791" :question/problem "There are three groups of fans in a certain town: those who prefer basketball, those who prefer baseball, and those who prefer football. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- from the people that prefer basketball, $32\\%$ change to baseball and $28\\%$ change to football,\n\n- from the people that prefer baseball, $45\\%$ change to football and $15\\%$ change to basketball,\n\n- from the people that prefer football, $25\\%$ change to basketball and $65\\%$ change to baseball.\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.15 & a \\\\ 0.32 & b & 0.65 \\\\ c & 0.45 & 0.1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each year:\n\n- From the people who prefer basketball, $32\\%$ change to baseball and $28\\%$ change to football. This implies that $40\\%$ of them continue to prefer basketball.\n\n- From people who prefer baseball, $45\\%$ change to football and $15\\%$ change to basketball. This implies that $40\\%$ of them continue to prefer baseball.\n\n- From people who prefer football, $25\\%$ change to basketball and $65\\%$ change to baseball. This implies that $10\\%$ of them continue to prefer football.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Basketball</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Baseball</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Football</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Basketball</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Baseball</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.32$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Football</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.28$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.45$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.15 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.32 & 0.4 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.28 & 0.45 & 0.1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.25+0.4+0.28=0.93.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "758ee170-1fcb-5640-8b61-f22fb7a21811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "520\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322063} {:answer/id #uuid "936daf2f-be59-5538-b600-77f0341e8b82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322316} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c760ab-015f-5f08-8419-73f7e6627df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "430\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322665} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8548e7-cae1-5bab-a94c-045833d4e0f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322760} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b6826a-9e7d-507c-8455-dfd824432ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "640\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322760} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe25491b-be23-5337-9805-76ec9cd44496" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323663}] :question/id #uuid "62389cec-4c75-591c-8976-ec4ed362e628" :question/math-academy-id "q-35845" :question/problem "A certain company produces two types of smartphones: Phone $A$ and Phone $B.$ The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of smartphones each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Phone $A$** | **Phone $B$** |\n| To: | **Phone $A$** | $0.6$ | $0.3$ |\n| **Phone $B$** | $0.4$ | $0.7$ |  |\n\nThat is, $40\\%$ of Phone $A$ customers switch to Phone $B$ each year, and $30\\%$ of Phone $B$ customers switch to Phone $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600\\,000$ customers use Phone $A,$ while $400\\,000$ customers use Phone $B.$ How many customers will use Phone $A$ in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.6 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each smartphone is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}600\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}\\begin{bmatrix}600\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.6 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.48 \\\\ 0.52\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $48\\%$ of customers will use Phone $A.$ Computing $48\\%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.48=480\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $480\\,000$ customers will use Phone $A$ in one year."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdf8c018-cb99-5053-b4e3-6368b7af2654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459933} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7a9fa2-a49a-52e6-a830-661568ed43fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459934} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7a0c060-bfe2-5f11-bc8e-da7a5a85d869" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460833} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "698591bc-2b07-574b-8dbc-ef38df909a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459935} {:answer/id #uuid "d1026046-c71f-597e-8a30-ff09f0697b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459935} :answer-field/id #uuid "db25fff1-1445-57ac-bff6-b3e390e53851" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460834} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0abbc15-e57c-54d7-8b6d-7db47e6feee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459937} {:answer/id #uuid "0f3dd321-b9d6-5e3c-b0b2-866e1fee6671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459937} :answer-field/id #uuid "38079a01-0d02-58da-86f9-5ca20a764db9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460835}] :question/id #uuid "3350b458-d932-58ce-9841-3d6f73360368" :question/math-academy-id "q-38977" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{4}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $T$ is invertible, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da97a065-c9ea-545d-9832-58ba5595c282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460017} {:answer/id #uuid "44b485c0-4b9e-522d-95c5-7ceba53f1f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460017} :answer-field/id #uuid "efff0b18-702b-56a0-8bdf-765f60a773b3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460875} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74ec198b-aef3-59b6-b091-30d662e9f4cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460019} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f8d74d-cce4-5aab-b18a-c61877075bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460020} :answer-field/id #uuid "9908404e-2262-51c4-b8db-2672114ba2d0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0734130-7b87-5408-bf8e-b097433b8280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460021} {:answer/id #uuid "b49d1b8e-67c2-5af5-8d46-21f059350ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460021} :answer-field/id #uuid "6141e94f-e3cc-500f-ba91-1f18aa4e01ca" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460877}] :question/id #uuid "a059dc0b-5296-5e25-93e6-3ffd38125f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-47556" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $[1,-1]^{T}\\notin \\text{Im}(T)$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$\n\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2$\n\n- **III.** $T$ is invertible\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{2}$ into ${R}^{2},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=2 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ does not lie in $\\text{Im}(T)$, then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2},$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bec134f4-8ac1-5fd3-a994-79031801df9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123603} {:answer/id #uuid "e52ece27-d671-527d-aac5-dac055efd06a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123604} {:answer/id #uuid "2e01b189-5d9a-582b-a3da-d616b84586a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123605} {:answer/id #uuid "32960b14-b04a-59a2-9224-0b8a82626700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123606} {:answer/id #uuid "5ffd9fe3-d1f5-5a30-b5aa-c6b01c0e6506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123607}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9eda024b-b844-536e-bf1a-f1efd2375bc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123602}] :question/id #uuid "f935b331-7ef7-5abb-b2c6-c78c4160f9a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-47586" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n> *If $A$ is a $n \\cdot n$ matrix and $det(A) \\ne 0$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n1. $A$ has no inverse\n2. $Null(A)$ has no basis\n3. $A$ has $n$ pivot columns"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06680c4f-a621-5dc3-875e-3896db2af04b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460023} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1cf54f-05d3-5550-8f3d-cdfa3137d5bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460023} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3156979-45c5-5d24-80c1-f665fce48ba8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460878} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88c8b9c9-048e-580b-90b5-0615fcacbfe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460025} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd76941-0308-57d6-9b7b-3ba6c48171e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460026} :answer-field/id #uuid "44325e4a-3b71-5949-9822-a4ed24085c3a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460879} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3989b63d-47f8-5757-bb11-6a685f029eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460027} {:answer/id #uuid "16bc9a89-2517-5c10-b196-866970cad27b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460027} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9665add-7abd-5377-b1f0-9d3eafc436be" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460880}] :question/id #uuid "7f4e1182-d195-5cca-bb1a-601ea1f9c921" :question/math-academy-id "q-47599" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ .*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7}$\n- **III.** $A$ has $5$ pivot columns\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $A$ is an $7\\times 7$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=7=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $det(A)=0$* $\\,\\times $ \n\nSince the matrix $A$ is invertible, it must be  $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then  $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7}.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7}.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $A$ has $5$ pivot columns.* $\\,\\times $ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=7,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $7$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128886 :question/id #uuid "9e92a0a5-0876-5767-ae40-b791fac08a2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-2180" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{v}$ be a non-zero vector from $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and let $A$ be the rotation-scaling matrix given by\n![](images/e5/e5399adbb72423b43aa6614c8ff4f912bed98b84174cb86e8a8df498e8bc4c70.png)\n\nThe description of $A\\mathbf{v}$ in terms of geometric transformations in the plane is given below. What are the missing entries?\n\n*To compute the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$, we rotate $\\mathbf{v}$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\theta=\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ and then scale the result by a factor of $r=\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We can find the scale factor using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{det(A)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent $A$ as a product of a rotation and a scaling matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain that\n![](images/19/198a02ee021615924bf6bd96474ec270d6900bf511d77c85b901563b891955b7.png)\n\nFinally, we conclude that the missing entries are $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ and $r=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94d86fff-dc68-5944-a11c-48b5842a3030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{π}{4}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127406} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7792a3-78c6-5228-b8d8-bf7959231100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{π}{2}$ and $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127407} {:answer/id #uuid "156830c5-d75d-56d6-8671-fd9377e49ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{π}{4}$ and $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127408} {:answer/id #uuid "31164ef4-74bf-5d28-92fd-b0bbb673e9ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{3π}{4}$ and $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127409} {:answer/id #uuid "4253b1a9-1225-55f8-adfe-507fb96644bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{π}{2}$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127410}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2f12ad06-ef79-57f6-a7a3-897a5b5c78db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127405}] :question/id #uuid "808610c0-b294-52e3-ba04-136434bdfcad" :question/math-academy-id "q-38473" :question/problem "Let $v$ be a non-zero vector from $R^{2}$ and let $A$ be the rotation-scaling matrix given by\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}5\\sqrt{2} & -5\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5\\sqrt{2} & 5\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nThe description of $Av$ in terms of geometric transformations in the plane is given below. What are the missing entries?\n\n> *To compute the vector $Av$, we rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $θ = \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ and then scale the result by a factor of $r = \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "066258b8-15e6-594c-9957-bef407a3a66f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 16 & 10 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557883} {:answer/id #uuid "2845ed2a-37d5-5090-83dd-454cf30d1240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 22 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557947} {:answer/id #uuid "882efe69-415d-52e7-90f5-e0920947df5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 8 \\\\ 16 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558134} {:answer/id #uuid "dca2cf6f-1869-5c13-97ce-b8ae8e71eb8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 16 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 22 \\\\ 16 & 10 & 30\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558303} {:answer/id #uuid "e141f47e-2de6-5ce4-9612-e71f3d7fadc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 16 & 10 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 16 & 10 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557883} :answer-field/id #uuid "cba59baf-234c-541c-bccf-8a885cebd27b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130840}] :question/id #uuid "fb487cc5-1cc1-5a9c-9d5a-d24c4773692c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139935" :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$8$** | **$16$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** | **$10$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$-10$** | **$-8$** | **$-5$** | **$-1$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $10$ | $16$ | $22$ | $30$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multi linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$8$** | **$16$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** | **$10$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$-10$** | **$-8$** | **$-5$** | **$-1$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $10$ | $16$ | $22$ | $30$ |\n\n \n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 16 & 10 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 22 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3afc1c8f-522d-5293-b5bc-fb0af445ea57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552990} {:answer/id #uuid "59f72235-cc0c-51ea-bfd1-ac4741215017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553062} {:answer/id #uuid "89f34cb4-3eca-51a1-a9e5-56975ac20a39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553145} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1b3757-35af-5e86-bd6c-bce2d443f2e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553282} {:answer/id #uuid "d58ff5f3-672a-5500-87ee-68ad8d33e95d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553145} :answer-field/id #uuid "15282714-ca78-5daf-8840-109d81c6e157" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132019}] :question/id #uuid "3c069118-41f8-5f1c-bae8-57e084a8f67d" :question/math-academy-id "q-40892" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "603e20fb-5fe2-5ee6-a1ec-6a40034bec5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 25 \\\\ 3 & 32 \\\\ 4 & 42 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551898} {:answer/id #uuid "a6fc93be-036b-5b3e-bc66-6cf227beda4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 25 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 32 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 42 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 15 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552066} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e540cd-3c04-55e6-a37b-b458bf640947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 25 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 32 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 42 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552111} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2cea96-c7d9-5a98-9f05-cc71f90c9189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552114} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3e0dd1-2a05-50c1-b114-ba517fd9603a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 25 \\\\ 3 & 32 \\\\ 4 & 42 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551898} :answer-field/id #uuid "69e5f672-7f5d-5b04-9df7-06332705d41d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132361}] :question/id #uuid "32203128-d23c-5cb0-ab0f-cdb995f482b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-42300" :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$25$** | **$32$** | **$42$** | **$15$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $7$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$25$** | **$32$** | **$42$** | **$15$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $7$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 25 \\\\ 3 & 32 \\\\ 4 & 42 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab6240c-a811-5ed5-a876-ef55a74dd515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550473} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5c471a-3743-58fa-b032-6489b39478a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550732} {:answer/id #uuid "aec9c374-1a4f-55d4-a0d4-1da3da5ab4af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550844} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd58429-3797-5a27-8e39-d9edfc6a019f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550979} {:answer/id #uuid "f6744f4c-62ff-5cd6-b5e9-04b41d3ac340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550732} :answer-field/id #uuid "f027d31b-b6f7-5925-b969-878c2b3df206" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132373}] :question/id #uuid "bb11277b-38b1-5736-b659-e34045c52325" :question/math-academy-id "q-42406" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85894a0e-794d-5a93-8322-05531ccb7ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558125} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cfb7b3-1243-56e5-be79-4ddbf8f16760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}^{T}(X{X}^{T})^{-1}Xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558185} {:answer/id #uuid "a7208c42-3174-5817-a288-64a14d6ba945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(X{X}^{T})^{-1}Xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558196} {:answer/id #uuid "b0432578-8555-51c5-a9b0-822d5e73f584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}^{T}\\,(X{X}^{T})^{-1}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558217} {:answer/id #uuid "e55b71c3-a6e4-5b02-a87a-8826a5be7062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558325} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fda705d-d1bb-56ec-97e8-e3c1b26c78ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132385}] :question/id #uuid "ecf29c4b-376c-5cb9-b877-1eb8940529e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-42409" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\beta =[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\nX\\beta =y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135062 :tutorial/content "Consider the following data set:\n\n| $x$ | **$100$** | **$110$** | **$120$** | **$130$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $12$ | $11$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n\nOur goal is to describe how to use matrices to carry out a linear regression. That is, we want to find an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\n![](images/8e/8ec830e3cf6154675487536afc0e09a42226eb83187b90238a0bfdcefeae5b38.png)\n\nLet's write our problem in matrix notation. Substituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+100{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+110{\\beta }_{1}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+120{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+130{\\beta }_{1}=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n![](images/8b/8bacbea6990395351aecca8befe779358d57d50f8c14f865d73c2107688fa728.svg)\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **design matrix,** and $\\mathbf{y}$ is called the **observation vector**. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a straight line, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nThis approach to finding parameters for a linear regression model is often called **ordinary least squares (OLS) regression**.\n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0.1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line of best fit (regression line) is \n\n$$\ny = 1+ 0.1x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "49c9c3ab-40d7-5e87-934c-1c3fcd16b330" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3652 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
